{"candidates":[{"content":"Replace the skeptical fixed-point over labels with a proof-theoretic grounded consequence relation: a node is accepted iff there exists a finite derivation tree using two rules — an attacker rule that discharges an attack when the attacker is refuted, and a warrant rule that introduces a committed root — and the extension is the set of formulas derivable in this monotone sequent calculus. The fixed point is recovered as the least Herbrand model of the derivation rules, preserving determinism and polynomial cost because derivability in a Horn fragment is P-complete; reinstatement is a derived theorem, not an extra postulate. This makes provenance internal to the derivation rather than an attached trace. Criticism: it may make the semantics less modular and harder to extend with quantitative information.","typicality":0.3,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_003","SRC_005"]},{"content":"Introduce a second dimension of 'suspension depth' as a primitive in the fixed-point operator, not as a post-pass: each node carries a counter equal to the minimum, over all attackers, of the maximum suspension depth of that attacker's defeaters; acceptance at depth d means the node is grounded after d rounds of simultaneous reinstatement. The operator is monotone on the product lattice of labels and bounded integers, so the least fixed point is unique and computable in polynomial time; reinstatement emerges as an increase in depth rather than a label change. This strengthens the extension by allowing a hierarchy of groundedness. Criticism: the choice of depth metric is arbitrary and may overfit to one notion of defense strength.","typicality":0.45,"neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Make the grounded extension conditional on a user-supplied 'commitment policy' that selects which suspended nodes may be used as defeaters for others, with the requirement that the policy be a monotone Boolean function over the attack graph. The semantics is the least fixed point of the operator parameterized by that policy; determinism is preserved because the policy is fixed before evaluation, polynomial cost is retained because the operator remains monotone, and validity of committed roots is unaffected because the policy cannot accept a node whose root is invalid. This opens a design space for policies that prioritize defense witnesses. Criticism: adding a policy parameter may introduce arbitrariness and require external justification for each policy.","typicality":0.55,"neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Abandon the single fixed point in favor of a set of admissible fixed points and define acceptance as membership in all of them (skeptical intersection), but compute it with alternating graph reachability: a node is accepted iff every maximal admissible set contains it, which is equivalent to the node being in the grounded extension by a known duality. This strengthens the semantics by making it robust to arbitrary changes in the attack graph and gives a natural proof of reinstatement as a set-inclusion property; complexity remains polynomial because admissible sets form an antimatroid and membership can be decided by two reachability queries. Criticism: the equivalence may rely on assumptions about finite graphs that do not hold for dynamic warrant generation.","typicality":0.35,"neighbours":["SRC_005","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Introduce a temporal dimension: define the grounded extension over a sequence of attack graphs where each step adds or removes edges, and require that the extension is the unique limit of the monotone fixed-point iterations over the sequence. This makes change first-class and allows a semantic definition of 'fragility' as the distance to the nearest graph in which a committed root is defeated; the cost is polynomial because each step uses the previous label as a warm start and the operator is monotone. This makes incremental caching a semantic, not merely an implementation, property. Criticism: the definition of distance between graphs is non-canonical and may affect the resulting extension.","typicality":0.25,"neighbours":["SRC_006","SRC_008"]},{"content":"Replace the binary attack relation with a quantitative defeat relation where each attack edge carries a cost, and define the grounded extension as the unique fixed point of a min-cost satisfiability problem: a node is accepted iff all attackers are refuted, and refutation of an attacker requires choosing a set of defeaters whose total cost is below a node-specific budget. This generalizes the closure rule to resource-bounded defense and preserves determinism via the least fixed point over the integer lattice; polynomiality holds for fixed budgets because it reduces to min-cut, and committed roots remain valid because budgets are derived from warrant strength. Criticism: quantitative costs may be hard to elicit and may not correspond to the qualitative nature of warrants.","typicality":0.4,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_007"]}]}